<section xml:id="sec-representation-of-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Representation of Vectors</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.2 Vectors
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Represent vectors geometrically on Cartesian plane
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Representing vectors geometrically on a Cartesian plane; equivalent vectors.
         Suggested activity: Learners draw vectors on a grid board from given column vectors and identify equivalent vectors.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Draggable arrow on a grid; its column vector is shown, and copies of the same vector can be placed anywhere to show equivalence. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Essential 2.2 (b)
         Activity: as planning notes (vectors drawn on a grid from columns; which arrows are equal)
         JSXGraph: fig-equal-vectors (assets/jsxgraph/equal-vectors-grid.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/03-representation-of-vectors/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a vector is drawn as an arrow; equal vectors have the same length and direction wherever they are drawn; the position vector of a point; <q>end minus start</q>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> draw a vector from a column at any starting point; read a column from an arrow or from two points; decide which arrows are equal.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find the vectors between places on a village map.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find that a column vector fixes a length and a direction but not a position.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give out grid boards or squared paper with axes from <m>-6</m> to <m>6</m>. After part (b), ask: <q>Are these three arrows the same vector?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-same-vector-different-places">
        <title>Same Vector, Different Places</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw axes from <m>-6</m> to <m>6</m> on squared paper. Let <m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw <m>\mathbf{a}</m> three times: starting at the origin <m>O</m>, at <m>(2, 3)</m> and at <m>(-4, -2)</m>. Where does each arrow end?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>At <m>(3, 1)</m>, <m>(5, 4)</m> and <m>(-1, -1)</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the same about the three arrows? What is different?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>They have the same length and the same direction; only their starting points differ.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><xref ref="fig-which-arrows-equal"/> shows four arrows: <m>\mathbf{p}</m> from <m>(1, -3)</m> to <m>(4, -2)</m>, <m>\mathbf{q}</m> from <m>(6, 0)</m> to <m>(3, -1)</m>, <m>\mathbf{r}</m> from <m>(-3, 2)</m> to <m>(-2, 5)</m> and <m>\mathbf{s}</m> from <m>(5, 2)</m> to <m>(8, 3)</m>. Which of them are equal to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\mathbf{p}</m> and <m>\mathbf{s}</m>, both <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>. <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m> is the reverse; <m>\mathbf{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m> has across and up swapped.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw the arrow from <m>O</m> to the point <m>A(-2, 4)</m> and write its column vector.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>: the same numbers as the coordinates of <m>A</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="fig-which-arrows-equal">
        <caption>Four arrows, each labelled at its tail.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
            <shortdescription>Four arrows on a grid: p from (1, −3) to (4, −2), q from (6, 0) to (3, −1), r from (−3, 2) to (−2, 5), s from (5, 2) to (8, 3).</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[very thin, gray!40] (-4,-4) grid (9,6);
\draw[->] (-4,0) -- (9.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-4,-2,2,4,6,8} \node[below] at (\x,0) {\scriptsize \x};
\foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4,6} \node[left] at (0,\y) {\scriptsize \y};
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (1,-3) -- (4,-2); \node[below left] at (1,-3) {$\mathbf{p}$};
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (6,0) -- (3,-1); \node[above right] at (6,0) {$\mathbf{q}$};
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (-3,2) -- (-2,5); \node[below left] at (-3,2) {$\mathbf{r}$};
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (5,2) -- (8,3); \node[below left] at (5,2) {$\mathbf{s}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise equal vectors, position vectors and the vector between two points.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (b) shows that where an arrow starts does not change the vector.</p></li>
                <li><p>A position vector starts at <m>O</m>, so its column is the coordinates of the point (part (d)).</p></li>
                <li><p>The vector from one point to another is <q>end minus start</q>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-equal-and-position-vectors">
        <title>Equal Vectors and Position Vectors</title>
        <p>
            <term>Equal vectors</term> (also called equivalent vectors) have the same length and the same direction, anywhere on the page. Equal vectors have the same column vector.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <term>position vector</term> of a point <m>A</m> is the vector from the origin to <m>A</m>: <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m>. Its column is the coordinates of <m>A</m>. For example, if <m>A</m> is <m>(-2, 4)</m> then <m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The vector from <m>A(x_A, y_A)</m> to <m>B(x_B, y_B)</m> is end minus start:
            <md>\overrightarrow{AB} = \begin{pmatrix} x_B - x_A \\ y_B - y_A \end{pmatrix}</md>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-equal-vectors">
        <caption>Drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> to change the vector. Drag the tail of the copy anywhere on the grid: it keeps the same column vector, because equal vectors can start anywhere.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-equal-vectors" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="5:6" source="jsxgraph/equal-vectors-grid.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-equal-vectors" surface="jsxboard" aspect="5:6"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Drag the tail of the dashed copy to three different places. Does its column change?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> so that <m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m>. What happens to the copy?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the tail of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> to the origin. The column of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is now the coordinates of its tip: a position vector.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-drawing-from-a-column">
        <title>Drawing from a Column</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw <m>\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m> starting at <m>(4, -1)</m>, and <m>\mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> starting at <m>(-3, 2)</m>. Where does each arrow end?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>From <m>(4, -1)</m>, move <m>2</m> left and <m>5</m> up: <m>\mathbf{b}</m> ends at <m>(2, 4)</m>.</p>
            <p>From <m>(-3, 2)</m>, move <m>4</m> right and not up or down: <m>\mathbf{c}</m> ends at <m>(1, 2)</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-which-vectors-equal">
        <title>Which Vectors Are Equal?</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The points are <m>A(1, 2)</m>, <m>B(4, 7)</m>, <m>C(-3, 0)</m>, <m>D(0, 5)</m>, <m>E(5, -1)</m>, <m>F(2, -6)</m>, <m>G(2, -5)</m> and <m>H(7, -2)</m>. Which of <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{CD}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{EF}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{GH}</m> are equal?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>End minus start for each:</p>
            <p><md>
                <mrow>\overrightarrow{AB} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 1 \\ 7 - 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}, \amp \overrightarrow{CD} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 0 - (-3) \\ 5 - 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix},</mrow>
                <mrow>\overrightarrow{EF} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 2 - 5 \\ -6 - (-1) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}, \amp \overrightarrow{GH} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 7 - 2 \\ -2 - (-5) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}.</mrow>
            </md></p>
            <p><m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{CD}</m>. <m>\overrightarrow{EF}</m> is the reverse of <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>, and <m>\overrightarrow{GH}</m> has across and up swapped, so neither is equal to <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-village-map">
        <title>Places on a Village Map</title>
        <statement>
            <p>On a village map the chief's office is the origin <m>O</m> and <m>1</m> unit is <m>1\,\text{km}</m>. The shop is at <m>S(5, 2)</m> and the clinic at <m>C(-1, 4)</m>. Write the position vectors of <m>S</m> and <m>C</m>, and the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{SC}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{CS}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>\overrightarrow{OS} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OC} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p><m>\overrightarrow{SC}</m> is end minus start: <m>\begin{pmatrix} -1 - 5 \\ 4 - 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>, that is <m>6\,\text{km}</m> west and <m>2\,\text{km}</m> north. <m>\overrightarrow{CS}</m> is the reverse: <m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example <c>-3</c>. Drag each point on the grid; it jumps to the nearest grid point. Every point must be moved before you check.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-drawing-a-vector-on-a-grid">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-drawing-a-vector-on-a-grid" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/03-representation-of-vectors/Drawing-a-vector-on-a-grid.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-which-arrows-are-equal-to-a">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-which-arrows-are-equal-to-a" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/03-representation-of-vectors/Which-arrows-are-equal-to-a.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-the-vector-between-two-points">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-the-vector-between-two-points" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/03-representation-of-vectors/The-vector-between-two-points.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <em>Vectors in everyday life:</em> a county map gives each place a position from a fixed point. Where would you choose the origin for a map of your village, and why?
    </p>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-representation-of-vectors">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-draw-from-the-origin">
            <statement><p>Draw these vectors starting at <m>O</m>: <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Give the end point of each.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>(2, 6)</m>, <m>(-4, 1)</m>, <m>(3, -3)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-draw-from-two-points">
            <statement><p>Draw <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> starting at <m>(1, -1)</m> and again starting at <m>(4, 4)</m>. Where does each arrow end?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>(-4, 1)</m> and <m>(-1, 6)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ab-from-coordinates">
            <statement><p><m>A</m> is <m>(2, -3)</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>(-1, 4)</m>. Write <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{BA}</m> as column vectors.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{BA} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-equal-vector-pairs">
            <statement><p>Find the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{RS}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{TU}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{VW}</m> for <m>P(0, 0)</m>, <m>Q(2, 3)</m>, <m>R(4, 1)</m>, <m>S(6, 4)</m>, <m>T(-2, 5)</m>, <m>U(1, 7)</m>, <m>V(3, -2)</m> and <m>W(5, 1)</m>. Which are equal?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{RS}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{VW}</m> are equal (all <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>); <m>\overrightarrow{TU} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-dispensary-and-borehole">
            <statement><p>On a map of a village near Kitui, the chief's camp is the origin and <m>1</m> unit is <m>1\,\text{km}</m>. The dispensary is at <m>D(4, -2)</m> and the borehole at <m>B(-3, 1)</m>. Write the position vectors of <m>D</m> and <m>B</m>, and the vector <m>\overrightarrow{DB}</m>. Describe <m>\overrightarrow{DB}</m> in words.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>; <m>\overrightarrow{DB} = \begin{pmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>7\,\text{km}</m> west and <m>3\,\text{km}</m> north.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-fourth-corner">
            <title>Challenge: The Fourth Corner</title>
            <statement><p><m>ABCD</m> is a parallelogram with <m>A(1, 1)</m>, <m>B(5, 2)</m> and <m>C(6, 5)</m>. Use <m>\overrightarrow{AD} = \overrightarrow{BC}</m> to find <m>D</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal vectors.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>D(2, 4)</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>\overrightarrow{BC} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 - 5 \\ 5 - 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. So <m>D</m> is <m>1</m> across and <m>3</m> up from <m>A(1, 1)</m>: <m>D(2, 4)</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-representation-of-vectors">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-equal-vectors"/> and activity parts (a) and (b). Work through <xref ref="example-drawing-from-a-column"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-draw-from-the-origin"/> and <xref ref="ex-draw-from-two-points"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-draw-from-the-origin"/> to <xref ref="ex-dispensary-and-borehole"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-fourth-corner"/>. Extension: find the other two points that make a parallelogram with <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>. (Answer: <m>(0, -2)</m> and <m>(10, 6)</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

